LECTURES ON LIBERATION (35 minutes)
• Key Concept: Freedom is not a "static, given principle" but a "dynamic, active struggle" for liberation.
• Discussion Point: Analyze Davis’s use of Frederick Douglass to argue that the "first condition of freedom" is the open act of resistance.
• Core Reading: Focus on her analysis of the Douglass-Covey fight, where the slave's resistance challenges the master’s very identity and reveals the master's dependence on the slave.
What's the difference between having consciousness of oppression and having consciousness of the necessity to abolish it? Why does this distinction matter?
-	"The authentic consciousness of an oppressed people entails an understanding of the necessity to abolish oppression."
Why is the slave actually "more enlightened" about what freedom means?
-	"The slave experiences the freedom of the master in its true light."
Why is resistance—not consciousness alone, but action—essential to freedom?
-	"The first condition of freedom is the open act of resistance."
What does she mean when she calls Covey both indispensable and marginal? What is he defending against?
-	Covey's "existence is the sine qua non of slavery" yet he finds himself "almost on the margin of slavery, the last barrier between physical enslavement and physical liberation."
What does this tell us about why the system needs the slave-breaker? What happens when the slave refuses to accept the assigned role?
-	When Douglass physically resists Covey, Davis observes: "That act of open resistance challenges his very identity... Covey implicitly recognizes the fact that he is dependent on the slave... for the affirmation of his own identity."**

AUTOBIOGRAPHY EXCERPT (25 minutes)
• Topic: Her 1970–1971 imprisonment and An Autobiography.
• Key Concept: The transition from "philosophical voyage" to the "reality of my situation".
• Discussion Point: How did Davis apply her theories of resistance while in solitary confinement? Contrast her "individualized predicament" with the collective "goodnight ritual" and solidarity from other prisoners.
• Core Reading: The excerpts on her hunger strike and the "prison within a prison" of solitary confinement.

Davis faces murder charges and the gas chamber, yet writes: "Yet, at that moment, I was feeling better than I had felt in a long time. The struggle would be difficult, but there was already a hint of victory." How is this possible? What is she experiencing?
In solitary confinement, when Davis hears the demonstration outside her window—"Free Angela! Free all our sisters!"—she writes she felt liberated. But then: "Reflecting upon the impenetrability of this fortress... I felt the weight of imprisonment perhaps more at that moment than at any time before."** What's happening in her consciousness in this moment? How does hearing the collective struggle intensify her awareness of isolation?
At lockdown, prisoners call out "goodnight" to each other across corridors in an unprecedented, prolonged ritual. Why does Davis dwell on this seemingly small moment? What is she demonstrating about resistance and collective consciousness?

POLITICAL PRISONERS, PRISONS & BLACK LIBERATION (40 minutes)
• Topic: “Political Prisoners, Prisons, and Black Liberation”
• Key Concept: Prisons as tools of "political repression" rather than "rehabilitation".
• Discussion Point: Essay on “Political Prisoners, Prisons, and Black Liberation”, “Statement to the Court,” “Prison, Where is Thy Victory?” writings to define the "political prisoner" as one who rejects the legitimacy of the system itself.

How is the prison positioned exactly like the slave-breaker—as a last line of defense? Against what?
-	Davis argues: "As the Black Liberation Movement and other progressive struggles increase in magnitude and intensity, the judicial system and its extension, the penal system, consequently become key weapons in the state's fight to preserve the existing conditions of class domination."**
What is she claiming about why the prison becomes more violent as consciousness and resistance increase?
-	Davis writes: "The ruling circles of America are expanding and intensifying repressive measures designed to nip revolutionary movements in the bud... The government is not hesitating to utilize an entire network of fascist tactics."** She then argues: "The sharp edge of political repression... must necessarily pour over into the contained world of the prison where it understandably acquires far more ruthless forms."**
What makes someone a political prisoner?
-	Davis distinguishes the political prisoner: "There is a distinct and qualitative difference between one breaking a law for one's own individual self-interest and violating it in the interests of a class or a people whose oppression is expressed either directly or indirectly through that particular law."** She notes that sometimes "the so-called crime does not even have a nominal existence."** 
SYNTHESIS: In the Lectures, Davis identifies the slave-breaker as "indispensable" yet "on the margin"—the last barrier to liberation. In the Political Prisoners essay, she argues the prison serves an analogous function as the state's most violent last line of defense against revolutionary consciousness. What does this parallel reveal about what the system is actually defending against? And what does it suggest political prisoners are capable of?
